"""Phase 5b copy, batch 2: business health, difficult client, objections, scope of practice. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase5_common import base, emit, s

D = {}

D["coaching-business-health-check"] = base(
    "coaching-business-health-check",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Business check-up",
    title="Coaching business health check",
    h1="Coaching business health check",
    description="Score your coaching business on admin, tools, onboarding, check-ins, retention, pricing, client experience and capacity, and see your biggest leaks.",
    intro="Answer eight quick questions about how your coaching business runs day to day, from admin time and tools to retention, prices and capacity. You'll get a score out of 16, the areas costing you the most and a practical fix for each. In most coaching businesses, the biggest gains come from fewer wasted hours and fewer lost clients rather than more leads. Your answers stay in your browser.",
    q="What makes a coaching business healthy?",
    a="Look beyond revenue at the habits that protect it. A healthy coaching business keeps admin under control, uses a small number of tools, onboards and checks in every client the same way, keeps clients for many months, reviews prices regularly, gives clients one clear place to find everything, and has room to grow. Each area scores 0 to 2, for a total out of 16.",
    sections=[
        s("Why these eight areas",
          "Coaching businesses rarely fail because the coach doesn't know enough about training or nutrition. They struggle because of what surrounds the coaching: hours lost to admin, clients who leave after a few months, a scattered client experience, prices that haven't moved in years, and a calendar that's full before the business is profitable. The eight questions cover the areas that most often decide whether a coaching business grows or stalls.",
          "Each area is simple to measure and possible to improve within a few weeks. That's what makes the check useful: it points to specific changes rather than a vague sense that things could be better."),
        s("How scoring works",
          ("table", ["Area", "2 points", "1 point", "0 points"], [["Admin time", "Under 3 hours a week", "3 to 8 hours", "8+ hours"], ["Tools", "1 to 2", "3 to 4", "5 or more"], ["Onboarding", "Structured and consistent", "A rough checklist", "Ad hoc"], ["Check-ins", "Structured, easy to scan", "Freeform but regular", "Inconsistent"], ["Client retention", "12+ months", "6 to 12 months", "Under 6 months"], ["Last price rise", "In the last year", "1 to 2 years ago", "Over 2 years or never"], ["Client experience", "One branded app", "A couple of tools", "Scattered"], ["Capacity", "Room to grow", "Near full", "Over capacity"]]),
          "The total out of 16 gives a band: 14 or more is strong, 10 to 13 solid, 6 to 9 leaky, and under 6 fragile."),
        s("How leaks are chosen",
          "Your biggest leaks are the areas that scored lowest. When several areas tie, the check uses a fixed priority: retention first, then capacity, admin, check-ins, onboarding, client experience, pricing and tools. Retention comes first because it compounds every other number: a business that keeps clients longer needs fewer new ones, earns more from each and has more time to improve everything else.",
          "The check shows up to three leaks. Fix the first before starting on the second; trying to fix everything at once usually means fixing nothing properly."),
        s("Retention: the leak that matters most",
          "If clients stay under six months on average, most of your effort goes into replacing them. Retention improves with a strong first 90 days, regular structured check-ins, visible progress and personal contact when clients go quiet. Even moving average retention from five months to eight increases lifetime value by 60% without a single new client.",
          "The client churn rate and LTV calculators on this site show what retention is worth in your business."),
        s("Admin and tools",
          "Admin hours grow quietly: answering the same questions, chasing check-ins, rebuilding plans, scheduling, invoicing. More than eight hours a week is a sign that systems could do more. Templates for programmes and common replies, batching check-in reviews and automating scheduling and payments can each save hours.",
          "Too many tools add to admin. Each extra app is another login for clients, another subscription and another place for information to get lost. Consolidating onto fewer tools usually makes the business both cheaper and easier to run."),
        s("Onboarding and check-ins",
          "Onboarding sets the tone for the whole coaching relationship. A structured process, with a welcome message, intake form, first call, first plan and a set first check-in date, makes every new client feel looked after and makes your job easier. Ad hoc onboarding means some clients get a great start and others get a forgotten one.",
          "Check-ins are where coaching actually happens each week. A short, consistent form with the same numbers and questions every time lets you review each client quickly and spot changes. Freeform check-ins take longer to read and make trends harder to see."),
        s("Pricing",
          "If you haven't raised your prices in over two years, your real income has probably fallen as costs have risen. Reviewing prices every year, against your costs, results and demand, keeps your business sustainable. The price increase calculator shows how many clients you could lose and still come out ahead."),
        s("Client experience",
          "When clients have to look in three places, a messaging app, a spreadsheet and an email, for their plan, logs and feedback, they're more likely to drift. One clear, branded place for everything makes coaching feel professional and easy to follow, which supports both results and retention."),
        s("Capacity",
          "A full calendar feels like success, but being over capacity means quality slips: slower replies, shorter feedback, missed details. If you're near or past capacity, raise prices for new clients, start a waiting list or bring in help before quality suffers. The client capacity calculator helps estimate your real limit."),
        s("Re-checking every quarter",
          "Run the check every three months. Note your score and leaks, fix the top one, then re-check. Over a year, most coaches see their score rise by several points, and the improvements usually show up in retention, hours and revenue."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What is a good score?", "14 or more out of 16 is strong; 10 to 13 is a solid base with a couple of leaks to fix."),
        ("Which leak should I fix first?", "The first one listed. Retention usually comes first, because it affects every other number."),
        ("Is my data saved?", "No. The check runs in your browser and nothing is stored or sent."),
        ("How often should I take the check?", "Every quarter, after fixing the top leak from the previous check."),
    ],
    pitch_key="client-app",
    pitch_h="Fix several leaks in one place",
    pitch="In Traiv, plans, nutrition, check-ins and chat sit in one app under your name. Fewer tools, a clearer client experience and less admin are often the fastest leaks to close.",
    keep=[("The client app in Traiv", "/features/client-app"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("Client capacity calculator", "/tools/client-capacity-calculator"), ("Client churn rate calculator", "/tools/client-churn-rate-calculator"), ("Coaching tool stack cost calculator", "/tools/coaching-tool-stack-cost-calculator")],
)

D["difficult-client-responder"] = base(
    "difficult-client-responder",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Client relationship",
    title="Difficult client responder for coaches",
    h1="Difficult client responder",
    description="Pick a difficult coaching situation and get the boundary that applies, the move to make and a warm but firm message you can adapt and send.",
    intro="Knowing what to programme is the easy part; knowing what to say to a demanding, upset or silent client is harder. Choose the situation to see the principle that applies, what to do and a kind, clear message to adapt. When the situation calls for a doctor, counsellor or other professional rather than a coach, it says so. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="How do I handle a difficult coaching client?",
    a="Name the boundary, stay kind and be specific. Most difficult situations, such as late-night messages, requests for discounts or free extras, or clients not doing the work, come from expectations that were never made clear. Restate the expectation warmly, offer a practical option and stick to it. If a situation involves a crisis, safety or abuse, it isn't a coaching conversation; refer or step back.",
    sections=[
        s("Why boundaries keep clients",
          "Many coaches avoid setting boundaries because they worry about losing the client. In practice, the opposite is usually true. Clients feel more secure when they know how coaching works: when you reply, what's included, what happens if they miss a session. Unclear boundaries lead to resentment on both sides, and resentment ends coaching relationships far more often than a kind, clear message does.",
          "The best boundaries are set at the start, in your onboarding and terms, and restated gently when needed."),
        s("The ten situations",
          ("table", ["Situation", "The boundary"], [["Messages at all hours", "Clear response hours, agreed at the start"], ["Wants faster results", "Honest timelines, not promises"], ["Not doing the work", "A plan realistic enough to follow"], ["Wants a refund", "Your refund terms, applied fairly"], ["Wants a discount", "One price for the same service"], ["Compares you to another coach", "Your approach, explained with confidence"], ["Wants extra, free", "What's included is written down"], ["Ghosted, now back", "A warm welcome back and a clear restart"], ["Off-loading a personal crisis", "Kindness within the limits of coaching"], ["Rude or inappropriate", "Respectful communication both ways"]]),
          "Each situation comes with a move to make and a message you can adapt. Change the wording so it sounds like you and mentions the client's specific circumstances."),
        s("Warm but firm",
          "Every message is written to hold a boundary without damaging the relationship. That means acknowledging the client's feelings or situation, stating the boundary clearly, explaining briefly why it exists, and offering a practical option. It avoids blame, sarcasm and long justifications.",
          "Firmness comes from clarity and consistency, not harsh words. If you state a boundary and then don't keep it, the boundary disappears. If you keep it kindly, clients learn to trust it."),
        s("Messages at all hours",
          "Clients who message late at night or on weekends usually aren't being difficult; they're messaging when they have time. The problem arises when you reply at those times, because it sets an expectation. Tell clients your reply hours at onboarding, set an auto-reply if your tools allow it, and reply during your hours. Offer a way to flag genuine urgency."),
        s("Discounts and free extras",
          "Discounts for one client are unfair to the others, and free extras quietly raise your workload without raising your income. Hold your price, and offer a lighter package if budget is the issue. For extras, explain what's included and offer the extra as a paid add-on or an upgrade. Most clients accept this readily when it's explained calmly."),
        s("Refunds",
          "Have clear refund terms written into your agreement, and apply them consistently. When a client asks for a refund, listen first: there may be a fixable problem. Then check the request against your terms. If it's fair, honour it gracefully; your reputation is worth more than one fee. Refund rules vary by country, so check what consumer law requires where your clients live."),
        s("When it's not a coaching situation",
          "Some situations go beyond coaching. A client sharing a personal crisis needs kindness, and often professional support you can't provide. A client who mentions self-harm or danger needs to be directed to emergency services or a crisis line straight away. A client who harasses or threatens you needs the coaching to end, with records kept. The responder flags these and says when to refer or step back."),
        s("Clients who aren't doing the work",
          "When a client consistently doesn't follow the plan, the instinct is to push harder. Usually the better move is to simplify. Ask what's getting in the way, without blame, and strip the plan back to one or two priorities. A plan the client actually follows beats a perfect plan they ignore."),
        s("Writing it into your onboarding",
          ("ul", [
              "Your reply hours and how to flag something urgent.",
              "What's included and what costs extra.",
              "Your cancellation, pause and refund terms.",
              "How check-ins work and when they're due.",
              "What you expect from the client, and what they can expect from you.",
          ]),
          "Clients who agree to these at the start rarely need the harder conversations later."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How do I set boundaries with coaching clients?", "At the start, in your onboarding and terms, then restate them warmly and consistently when needed."),
        ("Should I ever give a discount?", "Avoid discounting the same service for one client. Offer a lighter package at a lower price instead."),
        ("What if a client shares a mental health crisis?", "Respond with kindness, take the pressure off the plan and encourage professional support. If there's any risk of harm, direct them to emergency services or a crisis line."),
        ("Is this legal advice?", "No. For contracts, refunds or safety concerns, check your own terms and the relevant law, and seek professional advice."),
    ],
    pitch_key="messaging",
    pitch_h="Keep conversations with the coaching",
    pitch="In Traiv, chat sits beside each client's plan and check-ins, with read receipts and an unread count, so replies stay organised within your hours and nothing gets missed.",
    keep=[("Chat in Traiv", "/features/messaging"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("Sales objection handler", "/tools/sales-objection-handler"), ("Client churn-risk reader", "/tools/client-churn-risk-reader"), ("Coaching scope-of-practice check", "/tools/scope-of-practice-check")],
)

D["sales-objection-handler"] = base(
    "sales-objection-handler",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Sales and leads",
    title="Sales objection handler for coaches",
    h1="Sales objection handler",
    description="Pick a coaching sales objection to see what it usually means, an honest response to give, and a follow-up if the lead still hesitates.",
    intro="Hesitation on a sales call is normal, and how you respond shapes the decision. Choose what the prospect said to see the concern usually behind it, an honest reply and a gentle follow-up for later. The aim is to help a good fit say yes with confidence and to let someone who isn't ready leave on good terms. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="How do I handle objections on a coaching sales call?",
    a="Treat each objection as a question. 'Too expensive' usually means the value isn't clear yet; 'I need to think about it' often means a question hasn't been answered. Acknowledge it, ask what's behind it, answer that honestly, and offer a clear next step. If the coaching genuinely isn't a fit, say so.",
    sections=[
        s("Objections are questions",
          "When a lead raises an objection, they're rarely saying no. More often, they're telling you what they're unsure about: the cost, the time, whether it will work, whether they can commit. Treating objections as questions changes the conversation. Instead of defending your offer, you help the person work out whether it's right for them.",
          "That approach is also more honest. Some people raise objections because coaching really isn't right for them now. Helping them reach that conclusion gracefully protects your reputation and often brings them back later."),
        s("The nine objections",
          ("table", ["Objection", "What it usually means"], [["Too expensive", "The value isn't clear yet, or they're comparing with something different"], ["Need to think about it", "A question hasn't been answered"], ["No time right now", "They imagine coaching takes more time than it does"], ["Need to ask my partner", "A real step, especially with shared finances"], ["I'll start after an event", "Genuine timing, or delaying a big decision"], ["Tried coaching before", "They're worried about wasting money again"], ["I can do it myself", "Sometimes true; often a consistency problem"], ["Not sure it'll work for me", "They want reassurance someone like them can succeed"], ["Just send me the info", "A polite way to end the conversation"]]),
          "Each comes with a response to say and a follow-up if they still hesitate."),
        s("Price objections",
          "Price is the most common objection, and it's rarely only about money. If someone can't see how coaching will change their situation, any price feels too high. Ask what they're comparing it with, then explain clearly what's included and how it addresses their goals. If budget really is the issue, offer a lighter option rather than a discount on the full package.",
          "Avoid dropping your price on the call. It undervalues your work, signals that prices are negotiable and is unfair to clients who paid full price."),
        s("'I need to think about it'",
          "This often means a specific concern hasn't been addressed. Ask kindly what they want to think through: the cost, the time, whether it will work. Many people will tell you, and answering that one question resolves the decision. If more time really is needed, settle on a follow-up date together and keep to it."),
        s("Time objections",
          "People often imagine coaching means hours in the gym every day. Explain how much time the plan actually takes, such as three 40-minute sessions a week and a few minutes of logging, and how it fits around their schedule. If now really isn't the time, offer to check back in a month."),
        s("When someone has tried coaching before",
          "A lead who has been let down by a coach before is cautious for a good reason. Ask what didn't work last time: a generic plan, little contact, unrealistic expectations. Then explain specifically what will be different with you, and be honest if the same problem could arise. That honesty builds more trust than any promise."),
        s("Following up without pressure",
          "Most decisions aren't made on the call. A short follow-up the same day, summarising what you discussed and the next step, helps people decide. A second follow-up a few days later catches those who meant to reply but didn't. After that, add them to your email list with permission and leave the door open.",
          "Pressure tactics, such as fake deadlines and countdown discounts, may close a few sales but tend to bring clients who leave early. Honest follow-up brings clients who stay."),
        s("Letting a poor fit go",
          "Not every lead should become a client. If someone needs medical treatment, wants something you don't offer or clearly can't commit right now, tell them kindly and suggest a better option. Turning away a poor fit protects your results, your time and your reputation, and the people you turn away often refer friends who are a better fit."),
        s("Practising responses",
          "Read the responses aloud and adapt them until they sound like you. Practise with a friend or record yourself. The goal isn't to memorise a script but to feel comfortable answering common concerns calmly. Confidence on the call comes from knowing you have an honest answer ready."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How do I respond when a lead says it's too expensive?", "Ask what they're comparing it with, explain what's included and how it meets their goals, and offer a lighter option if budget is the issue."),
        ("What if they need to think about it?", "Ask what they'd like to think through. If they still need time, agree a day to follow up."),
        ("Should I offer a discount to close a sale?", "Generally no. Offer a smaller package instead; discounting the same service undervalues it and is unfair to other clients."),
        ("Is it ever right to turn a lead away?", "Yes, when coaching isn't the right fit. Doing it kindly protects your results and reputation."),
    ],
    pitch_key="client-app",
    pitch_h="Show leads what coaching looks like",
    pitch="Objections fade when leads can see what they'll get. With Traiv, you can show a prospect the branded app where their plan, check-ins and chat will live, so the coaching feels real before they decide.",
    keep=[("The client app in Traiv", "/features/client-app"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("Discovery call calculator", "/tools/discovery-call-calculator"), ("Difficult client responder", "/tools/difficult-client-responder"), ("Free trial conversion calculator", "/tools/free-trial-conversion-calculator")],
)

D["scope-of-practice-check"] = base(
    "scope-of-practice-check",
    eyebrow="Free tool · Safety",
    title="Coaching scope-of-practice check",
    h1="Coaching scope-of-practice check",
    description="Pick a client situation to see whether to refer out now, proceed only with clearance or coach as normal, with a referral message and a file note.",
    intro="Some client situations belong with a coach, some need a clinician's sign-off first, and some need a different professional entirely. Choose the situation to see which group it falls into, plus a message you can send and a short note for your records. It errs on the side of caution and is general guidance rather than legal or medical advice. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="When should a coach refer a client out?",
    a="Refer out when a situation needs diagnosis or treatment: undiagnosed or worsening pain, disordered eating, a diet to treat a disease, significant mental health concerns, acute medical issues and medication questions. Proceed only with clearance for pregnancy, older adults with health conditions, controlled chronic conditions, weight-loss medication and returning injuries. Healthy adults are in your lane.",
    sections=[
        s("What scope of practice means",
          "Scope of practice is the range of things you are trained, qualified and, in some places, legally allowed to do. For most fitness coaches and personal trainers, that means designing exercise programmes, giving general healthy eating guidance and coaching habits for healthy people. It doesn't include diagnosing conditions, treating injuries, prescribing diets for disease or giving medication advice.",
          "The exact boundaries depend on your qualifications and where you work. A registered dietitian or physiotherapist can do things a personal trainer can't. Always work within your own certification's scope and your local regulations."),
        s("The three calls",
          ("table", ["Call", "Meaning", "Examples"], [["Refer out now", "Outside coaching; needs a qualified professional", "Undiagnosed pain, disordered eating, therapeutic diets, acute medical issues"], ["Proceed only with clearance", "Coaching can continue within a clinician's guidance", "Pregnancy, controlled conditions, cleared injuries, weight-loss medication"], ["In your lane", "General coaching for healthy adults", "General training, general nutrition and habits, beginners"]]),
          "The check is cautious by design: when in doubt, it leans towards referral or clearance."),
        s("Why referring protects everyone",
          "Referring a client isn't a failure. It shows professionalism, protects the client's health and protects you. A coach who keeps training through undiagnosed pain or offers diet advice for a medical condition risks harm to the client and serious consequences for their business. A coach who refers appropriately builds trust with clients and with other professionals, who may refer clients back."),
        s("Clearance situations",
          "Some clients can train safely with the right guidance. Pregnant and postpartum clients, older adults with health conditions, people with controlled chronic conditions such as cleared diabetes or blood pressure, clients returning from an assessed injury, and those on weight-loss medication often benefit greatly from coaching. Ask for clearance from their doctor, midwife or physiotherapist, find out any restrictions, and coach within them.",
          "Keep a note of the clearance and any guidance, and review it if the client's situation changes."),
        s("Situations to refer",
          ("ul", [
              "**Undiagnosed or worsening pain:** pause painful movements until it's assessed.",
              "**Disordered eating signs or history:** avoid calorie targets and weighing; involve a doctor or specialist.",
              "**A diet to treat a disease:** that's for a registered dietitian or doctor.",
              "**Significant mental health concerns:** encourage professional support; share crisis contacts if there's risk.",
              "**Acute or uncontrolled medical issues:** stop training until a doctor has cleared them.",
              "**Medication or supplement questions:** refer to a doctor or pharmacist.",
          ]),
          "In each case, the message is kind, clear and focused on getting the client the right help."),
        s("Health screening at the start",
          "A health questionnaire at onboarding catches most situations before they become problems. Ask about medical conditions, medication, injuries, pregnancy, recent surgery and any symptoms during exercise. Use a recognised pre-exercise screening tool where possible, and refer for clearance when the answers suggest it.",
          "Ask clients to tell you if anything changes, and recheck the questionnaire periodically."),
        s("Keeping a record",
          "A brief file note for each referral or clearance protects you and the client. Record the date, what the client told you, what you advised, and any clearance or guidance received. The check provides a one-line note you can adapt. Store notes securely and in line with data protection rules."),
        s("Supporting clients after referral",
          "Referring doesn't have to end the coaching relationship. Many clients can continue training, adapted to their clinician's advice, while they get the help they need. Stay in touch, adapt the plan as guidance comes in, and let the client know you're there when they're ready. That continuity often matters a great deal to them."),
        s("Building a referral network",
          "Get to know a few local or online professionals you trust: a physiotherapist, a registered dietitian, a GP practice, a mental health service. Knowing who to refer to makes the conversation easier for the client and smoother for you. Many professionals welcome coaches who refer appropriately and may send clients your way in return."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("Can a personal trainer give nutrition advice?", "General healthy eating guidance for healthy people is usually within scope. Diets to treat medical conditions should come from a registered dietitian or doctor."),
        ("Should I train a client with pain?", "Not through undiagnosed or worsening pain. Pause those movements and refer them for assessment."),
        ("Can I coach clients on weight-loss medication?", "Yes, with their prescriber's guidance. Coach training and habits, and refer medication questions to the prescriber."),
        ("Is this legal advice?", "No. It's general, cautious guidance. Your certification's scope and local regulations come first."),
    ],
    disclaimer="General professional guidance only, not legal or medical advice. Your qualification's scope and local regulations come first. If a client describes severe or sudden symptoms, they should contact local emergency services.",
    nxt=[("GLP-1 client coaching planner", "/tools/glp1-client-coaching-planner"), ("Injury exercise swap finder", "/tools/injury-exercise-swap"), ("Difficult client responder", "/tools/difficult-client-responder")],
)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from phase5_b2_extra import EXTRA

